---
import Base from '~/layouts/Base.astro';
import Crossref from '~/components/primitives/Crossref.astro';
import Authorship from '~/components/primitives/Authorship.astro';

const description =
  'Why ERCs, Solved exists, who writes it, and how to contribute. An independent, spec-grade reference for the LUKSO LSP standards.';
---

<Base title="About" description={description}>
  <article class="article">
    <header class="page-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">colophon</span>
      <h1>About this field manual.</h1>
    </header>

    <section class="prose">
      <p class="lede">
        ERCs, Solved is a reference for the standards LUKSO designed instead of layered. It is
        written for the EVM developer who already knows what is broken on Ethereum and wants the
        decision support to move — verdict-first, not marketing-first.
      </p>

      <h2>The stance, in one paragraph.</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>ERCs are interfaces. LSPs are the system.</strong> ERC-20, 721, and 1155 won by
        underspecifying — they handed everyone a balance sheet and a transfer function and went
        home. Every team since has paid the integration tax: approval phishing, dead NFTs in
        vaults, tokenURI servers that go offline, EOAs nobody can recover, 4337 stacks bolted on
        to fake what an account should be. LSPs aren't a competing token spec — they're the
        surrounding system: receiver awareness, profile metadata, key-scoped permissions, relay
        execution, contract extension. This site exists to make that decision legible.
      </p>

      <h2>Editorial rules.</h2>
      <ul class="rules">
        <li>
          <span class="n">01</span>
          <span>One claim per page. The verdict goes above the fold.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="n">02</span>
          <span>No exclamation points. No "powerful." No "next-generation." If a standard needs a marketing word it isn't ready.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="n">03</span>
          <span>Every standard explainer includes a <em>what it does not solve</em> section. Anti-overselling is a feature.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="n">04</span>
          <span>Every page lists the author, the last-updated date, and an edit-on-github link.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="n">05</span>
          <span>We link to <code>docs.lukso.tech</code> for the canonical spec. We do not duplicate the spec — we make the decision around it.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How content is organized.</h2>
      <p>
        Two entry models converge on the same library: enter by the ERC you use today through
        <Crossref href="/problems/" chip="hub">Problems</Crossref>, or enter by what you're building
        through <Crossref href="/build/" chip="hub">Build</Crossref>. Both route to
        <Crossref href="/standards/" chip="hub">Standards</Crossref> explainers and side-by-side
        <Crossref href="/standards/compare/erc20-lsp7/" chip="LSP·7">comparisons</Crossref>.
        The single most-linkable page is the
        <Crossref href="/standards/matrix/" chip="LSP·*">ERC ↔ LSP matrix</Crossref>.
      </p>

      <h2>Independence.</h2>
      <p>
        This is an independent reference. It is unaffiliated with the LUKSO foundation. We don't
        publish anything we wouldn't ship in production ourselves, and we link to the official
        docs from every page so the spec stays canonical there.
      </p>

      <h2>Contribute.</h2>
      <p>
        Found a bug, a sharper verdict, a missing pattern? Every page has an{' '}
        <em>edit on github</em> link. Open a PR — issues are also fine. The content collection
        schemas are typed; reviewers will catch shape errors before merge.
      </p>
    </section>

    <Authorship
      author="ercs-solved maintainers"
      updated="2026-06-22"
      editHref="https://github.com/lukso-network/ercs-solved/edit/main/src/pages/about.astro"
    />
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .article {
    max-width: var(--container);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--s-8) var(--s-5);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-7);
  }
  .page-head {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
  }
  .page-head h1 {
    font-size: clamp(36px, 6vw, var(--t-serif-3));
  }

  .prose {
    max-width: var(--reading);
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-5);
  }
  .prose .lede {
    font-size: var(--t-mono-lg);
    color: var(--ink-bone);
  }
  .prose h2 {
    font-size: var(--t-serif-1);
    margin-top: var(--s-4);
  }
  .prose p { color: var(--ink-bone-dim); }
  .prose em { color: var(--ink-bone); font-style: italic; }
  .prose code {
    background: var(--ink-paper);
    border: 1px solid var(--ink-line);
    padding: 0 4px;
    font-size: 0.95em;
    color: var(--signal-pink);
  }

  .rules {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
  }
  .rules li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 32px 1fr;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    padding: var(--s-3) 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--ink-line);
    color: var(--ink-bone-dim);
  }
  .rules li:last-child { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--ink-line); }
  .rules .n {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-mono-sm);
    color: var(--signal-pink);
    letter-spacing: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .article { padding: var(--s-6) var(--s-4); }
  }
</style>
